import{_ as n,P as o,d as i,e as a,y as r,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const v=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.3 Orientation Anchor (HV03)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.3 Orientation Anchor (HV03)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1091,"xkLessons":{"A.3":{"title":"A.3 Orientation anchor (HV03)","lead":"The variable “Orientation Anchor” retains sourced expressions of meaning and heterogeneous goals; only when the outcome preselected for a “Meaning and Coordination” test receives support is a bounded effective anchor determined. Effects prove neither shared inner states nor goal justification.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3950–4033; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-03#section-a-3. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Short quotations: “candidate material about meaning” precedes a “conditionally effective orientation anchor.” Goals, identities, memories, commitments, fears, and common problems may all be sourced meaning materials, but how they alter resources, actions, or coordination requires separate evidence. A widespread slogan is not necessarily effective, still less proof of identical inner states or a justified goal. [T-main]","Route one (candidate anchor) requires traceable expressions and forms of carriage without an eligible “Meaning and Coordination” test yet, permitting the recording of heterogeneous expressions, representational disputes, and the need for a further test of this kind. The conditions for suspension prohibit a determination under route two when there are only slogans, interpreter projection, or coerced agreement, while explicitly retaining route one’s record of sourced expressions, heterogeneity, and coercion risks. Insufficient strong support cannot be rewritten as no materials to analyze. [T-main]","Route two (effective anchor) requires a pre-registered “Meaning and Coordination” test to receive support on the uniquely preselected criterion among resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes, and further requires the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals.” The outcome family among these three must be selected beforehand: if resources do not change, one cannot switch to actions, then select a favorable coordination measure when actions do not change. If multiple goals, models, or comparison rules need combining, fix them in advance too, rather than fishing for significance after results. [T-main；T-H1-contract]","Route two supports a conditional anchor only for this test, selected outcome family, scale, and time window. It does not extrapolate to unselected outcomes, imply genuine consent or unified inner states, or justify coercive meaning. An effective direction of coordination may arise from fear or pressure, making the distinction between established effects and normative justification especially important. Results that fail the positive criterion and remain “unresolved” or “not supported” also cannot declare meaning incapable of affecting any outcome without passing the null-conclusion criterion. [T-main；T-H1-contract]","Across nine axes, preserve differences between original expressions, action sequences, distributions of positions, and summaries. Aggregation requires added representation rules, heterogeneity, cost/benefit distributions, and scope of authorization. Different goals at low-safety positions cannot be erased by majority summaries; silence, coerced agreement, and internal competition enter uncertainty and local exclusion zones. Different people may use the same words while referring to different costs and actions. [T-main]","Time windows distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memories. Outputs may enter events, mobilization for uptake, and normative agendas, but those downstream uses cannot inherit an effect not yet proved. Those claiming representativeness and those allocating resources on that basis have separate recording responsibilities; the variable authorizes neither unified meaning, representation of wishes, nor coordinated action. [T-main]","Appeals permit denial of representativeness, presentation of heterogeneous goals, refusal to be anchored, and review outside the original judgment chain. Authorized restoration must actually revoke naming and its representative/downstream coordination effects, restore heterogeneous-expression status, leave a record of the changes, and verify completion; it cannot compress public opinions into another new slogan. [T-main]","The verifiable original 1990 ADA signing statement publicly invokes liberty, equality, and commitments to administrative implementation. This is sourced expressive material, but it does not itself provide a preselected-outcome effect relative to comparison conditions. The statement alone cannot establish support from a “Meaning and Coordination” test or universal public consent. Real historical materials need not be denied significance, but inference ceilings must remain. [P-ADA；T-main]","Item-by-item study (“Source text” below retains the item’s complete content; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Code. The source text gives the code only in parentheses at the end of the heading and uses the name in the text. Interpretation: the code is a short identifier within the code system of Appendix B; it tracks the same variable and does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified code. The source text does not list this item separately. Interpretation: the qualified form serves only to avoid collisions between identical names; references to this variable must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name. Source heading: Orientation Anchor. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Proposition role. The source text does not list this item separately; under §2.2 it belongs to the role of “human-domain clauses.” Interpretation: this role marks only that it applies to the human domain; it does not mean all six human-domain clauses are the same kind of fact. Route two effects of this variable require the support of an independent “Meaning and Coordination” test; the variable itself carries no empirical truth value. [T-main]","05 | Role in the system. The source text lists it as one of the eleven human variables of Part 7 (§7.3). Interpretation: this is the definition of a human variable, specifying how it enters analysis; listing the items does not establish that any empirical test holds. [T-main]","06 | Proposition. Source text: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem may first be recorded as candidate material about meaning. A conditionally effective orientation anchor is determined, within the scope of a “Meaning and Coordination” test (§4.2), only if that test provides support on the preselected outcome for resource allocation, action choice, or coordination. Interpretation: read this proposition together with the subsequent support routes and ceilings; do not extract an unconditional causal or normative conclusion from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope of application. Source text: Human structures involving intentionality, coordination, or common problems. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Conditions for suspension. Source text: Do not determine a conditionally effective orientation anchor under route two when there are only slogans, an interpreter’s projections, or coerced uniformity of expression. Sourced expressions, diverse expressions, and risks of coercion may still be recorded under route one. Do not infer a common will from them. Interpretation: pausing route two does not delete expressive evidence. Slogans or forced agreement alone do not determine an effective anchor, but sourced materials, heterogeneity, and coercion risks may remain under route one. [T-main]","09 | Inferential premises. Source text: Structural domain (§7.3.1). Interpretation: inferential premises provide upstream content needed for the proposition to hold; upstream candidate or support status must pass through unchanged, without automatic upgrading by citation. [T-main]","10 | Method requirements. Source text: Observation Position and Model Limits (§4.8.2); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7). Interpretation: method requirements constrain evidence gathering and expression; meeting method requirements does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Domain restriction. Source text: None. Interpretation: writing “none” means only that no such domain restriction is listed, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a listed restriction also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Objects of application. Source text: None. Interpretation: objects of application cannot replace the current object’s definition; writing “none” is an explicit statement, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Support routes. Source text: Route one, candidate anchor. Conclusion level: Candidate description. Conditions: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem has traceable ways of being expressed and carried, but there is not yet an eligible “Meaning and Coordination” test. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record candidate material about meaning, diverse expressions, disputes over representativeness, and the need for a further “Meaning and Coordination” test. Ceiling: Candidate expressions of meaning only. They must not be called an effective orientation anchor or a common will. Route two, effective anchor. Conclusion level: Conditional effect. Conditions: A pre-registered “Meaning and Coordination” test receives support on the single criterion selected in advance among resource allocation, action choice, and coordination outcomes. Further inferential premises: This “Meaning and Coordination” test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine a conditionally effective orientation anchor within this test, outcome family, scale, and time window. Ceiling: Do not extrapolate to unselected resource, action, or coordination outcomes. Do not infer genuine consent or a unified inner state, or treat the result as grounds for imposing uniformity of meaning. Interpretation: route one retains traceable meaning expressions and heterogeneous disputes; route two must uniquely preselect an outcome criterion among resources, actions, and coordination, have an eligible “Meaning and Coordination” test receive support, and further require the conditions for causal claims and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals,” without switching endpoints afterward. [T-main]","14 | Can infer. Source text: Conditional directions of coordination and anchors of conflict. Interpretation: coordination directions or conflict anchors may be judged only within the corresponding route’s scope; candidate expressions are not route two effects and do not automatically have normative justification. [T-main]","15 | Cannot infer. Source text: That a group has a unified inner state; that shared language amounts to genuine consent; that the goal is normatively justified. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale. Source text: Aggregation level covers individual expressions or actions, cases of particular subjects, the population of potential participants, distributions of stances, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of relationships, organizational spaces, digital public spaces, and the reach of propagation across domains. Time span covers the window from expression to action, cycles of commitment, and lags in drift and decoupling. Organizational level covers roles in action, teams, organizations, institutions, and public governance ecologies. Causal level covers events of expression, mechanisms of interaction between meaning and action, meso-level coordination structures, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original expressions and actions, event sequences, individual cases, distributions of stances, coordination metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct participants, dissenters, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences for coordination, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers propagation of expressions, links for coordination, heterogeneous clusters, and bridging nodes. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for naming the anchor, adopting a judgment of representativeness, and requiring coordination or uniformity separately. Diverse anchors must be preserved. Interpretation: state all nine axes individually; in particular, scope of influence creates no authority on the axis of jurisdiction and authorization, and lower observation resolution requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object. Source text: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem that may change resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes. Interpretation: this describes the variable’s corresponding object; choosing a lower-threshold descriptive route cannot treat functional meaning in the name as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Kept across scales. Source text: The bridge from meaning to the preselected resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcome. Interpretation: what is kept across scales is a comparative responsibility to be maintained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Must be added when moving to a larger scale. Source text: Rules of representation; heterogeneity; distribution of costs and benefits; scope of authorization. Interpretation: moving to a larger scale is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changes with scale. Source text: The content and strength of an anchor and the subjects who carry it can change. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Objects to which it does not apply. Source text: Nonhuman systems without intentionality, meaning, or capability for commitment. Interpretation: the inapplicability restriction prevents unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential concepts; it does not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Cannot be raised to. Source text: Local expression taken directly as a common will. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | States. Source text: Dispersed; coalescing; competing; solidified; decoupled. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require time windows, grounds, and records of changes. [T-main]","24 | Observables. Source text: Differences in resource allocation before and after the pre-registered expression of meaning appears; changes in action choices, rates of completion in collaboration, or conflict patterns; acceptance, rejection, and alternative expressions of the anchor at different positions; whether the outcome difference under the comparison conditions exceeds the predetermined threshold. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements. Source text: Resource adjustments; sequences of action; commitment and exit records; evidence of conflict. Interpretation: resource, action, commitment/exit, and conflict materials may have different uses; applying for the support of a “Meaning and Coordination” test requires keeping the preselected outcome family rather than retrospectively seeking the most favorable endpoint among them. [T-main]","26 | Inputs. Source text: Positions of participation; safety of expression; data on resources and actions. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between variables. [T-main]","27 | Outputs. Source text: Generative events (§7.2.3); mobilization of uptake; agendas for normative choice. Interpretation: outputs identify downstream judgments that may depend on this variable; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag. Source text: Distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memory. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty. Source text: Record silence, coerced agreement, and internal heterogeneity. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone. Source text: Different goals at positions where people have limited safety must not be erased in aggregation. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions. Source text: People who identify with the anchor; dissenters; people represented; cost bearers. Interpretation: record directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers. Source text: Narratives; commitments; shared memory; institutional goals; problem definitions. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties. Source text: Those making claims of representativeness; those allocating resources on that basis. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status. Source text: The existence of an anchor does not establish that it is normatively justified. Interpretation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the detailed description. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling. Source text: Limit the two routes separately. Route one only describes candidate expressions of meaning. When the corresponding “Meaning and Coordination” test supports the effect on the selected outcome, route two may explain a conditional anchor within a limited scope. Do not extrapolate to unselected outcomes. Interpretation: route one identifies only candidate expressions; route two is confined to the selected outcome effect. Improved coordination cannot establish improved resources or genuine consent. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling. Source text: This variable produces only descriptions of candidate anchors, diverse expressions, comparison results, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize unifying meaning, representing others’ wishes, or coordinating action. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures. Interpretation: The variable itself creates no real-world authority to act; actual actions separately check choice, normative bridging, explicitly stated normative premises, valid itemized authorization, operational procedures, protection, and duration. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples. Source text: A repeated slogan does not change any allocation of resources or action; uniform expressions under high pressure conceal genuine goals that conflict. Interpretation: Counterexamples limit unconditional extrapolation. They may require narrowing the scope or collecting evidence again, without automatically negating all bounded uses. [T-main]","38 | Appeal. Source text: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), members can contest representativeness, put forward different goals, or refuse to be anchored through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original chain of judgment about the anchor. Interpretation: Appeals must be safely reachable, protected against retaliation, and able to trigger independent review; a channel name alone does not establish power to change an error. [T-main]","39 | Restoration. Source text: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline actually revoke the naming of the anchor, remove its force as a representation and its downstream effect on coordination, and restore the state of diverse expression. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified. Interpretation: Within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion. Changing document wording does not mean rights and resources have been restored to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Fix the object, sources of expression, carrier forms, and observation positions.","Route one records candidate meanings, heterogeneous expressions, disputes about representation, and evidence needs.","When testing route two, first uniquely preselect one outcome family—resources/action/coordination—and its criteria.","Use a qualified “Meaning and Coordination” test, the conditions for causal claims, and “Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals” to judge the effects of this test, keeping unresolved and null conclusions distinct.","Restrict claims to the selected outcome window; do not infer consent, a common will, or normative justification.","Protect those refusing representation; after authorization, revoke erroneous downstream representative/coordination effects."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Expression of meaning","leftText":"Sourced objectives, fears, or commitments.","rightTitle":"Effective anchor","rightText":"A specified “Meaning and Coordination” test supports the preselected outcome effect."},{"leftTitle":"Common language","leftText":"People visibly say the same words.","rightTitle":"Genuine consent","rightText":"Requires checking heterogeneity, coercion, and free choice."},{"leftTitle":"Coordination effect","leftText":"A particular outcome changes under specified conditions.","rightTitle":"A justified objective","rightText":"Normative reasons and protection require separate judgment."},{"leftTitle":"The positive criterion was not met","leftText":"Unsupported or unresolved.","rightTitle":"Null conclusion","rightText":"Requires meeting a separately specified null-conclusion criterion; it cannot be automatically expanded."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the source text: a repeated slogan does not change any allocation of resources or action.","Counterexample in the source text: uniform expressions under high pressure conceal genuine goals that conflict.","Teaching counterexample: after a preselected resource outcome fails, switching to an attractive coordination metric to declare success.","Boundary in the source: local expressions cannot be directly elevated into a common will."],"questions":["Does a repeatedly occurring slogan constitute an effective anchor?","Does improved coordination establish that an objective is justified?","When coerced uniformity is found, should even the expressions go unrecorded?","Can another favorable outcome rescue a failed preselected outcome?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-routes.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"The 1990 ADA signing statement as a traceable expression","href":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"The 1990 ADA signing statement as a traceable expression","text":"The presidential statement of 1990-07-26 publicly expresses ideals of freedom and equality, administrative support, and commitments to implementation; the archives preserve its transcript.","sourceLabel":"US National Archives: Statement By The President, July 26, 1990","sourceUrl":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html","sourceLocator":"The statement's opening paragraph and final two body paragraphs, webpage lines 27 and 41–42","boundary":"The text establishes only the expression's content and timing, not fulfillment of commitments, the effects of meaning and coordination, public consent, or full justification of the objectives.","href":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.3","start_line":4007,"end_line":4090,"startLine":4007,"endLine":4090}],"outputId":"section-a-3","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: three outcomes cannot be selected for the best result afterward","text":"A constructed study preselects resource allocation alone as its primary outcome. It later observes a resource difference of 0, an action-metric difference of 2, and a coordination-metric difference of 8. The apparent size of coordination's 8 cannot justify renaming the original resource test a successful “Meaning and Coordination” test. Coordination material may generate a separately pre-registered candidate hypothesis; whether the resource result supports a null effect still requires its dedicated null-conclusion criterion, and a difference of 0 alone is insufficient.","assumptions":"Numbers, metric scales, and the study are teaching constructions; no formal statistical determination is supplied.","boundary":"Failure to support one primary outcome does not negate every effect of meaning. What is prohibited is changing the primary target after results are known."},{"title":"Teaching construction: different objectives beneath uniform expression","text":"In a fictional meeting, everyone says “protect the project.” A hopes to keep a job, B hopes to stop a dangerous function on schedule, and C fears retaliation for dissent. Shared wording and heterogeneous interpretations may be recorded, without inferring unified inner intentions from identical wording. Nor may C be forced to publicly prove their situation before a safety gap is recognized.","assumptions":"Constructed people and situations; no inference about actual motives.","boundary":"Different interpretations require support from the person concerned or independent material; analysts cannot project actual inner states themselves."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: a repeated slogan does not change any allocation of resources or action.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the source text: uniform expressions under high pressure conceal genuine goals that conflict.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: after a preselected resource outcome fails, switching to an attractive coordination metric to declare success.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the source: local expressions cannot be directly elevated into a common will.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does a repeatedly occurring slogan constitute an effective anchor?","answer":"It suffices only to record expression under qualified sourcing; route two requires a corresponding “Meaning and Coordination” test to support the preselected outcome.","basis":"Route one and route two."},{"question":"Does improved coordination establish that an objective is justified?","answer":"No. Effects and normative standing remain separate.","basis":"“Normative status” and “Cannot infer.”"},{"question":"When coerced uniformity is found, should even the expressions go unrecorded?","answer":"Record sourced expressions and coercion risks, but do not determine an effective anchor under route two or a common will.","basis":"The “Conditions for suspension” item."},{"question":"Can another favorable outcome rescue a failed preselected outcome?","answer":"The outcome family cannot be changed after the event; new discoveries retain exploratory status.","basis":"The provisions for “Meaning and Coordination” tests (§4.2) and route two."}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-routes.png","alt":"Route one, candidate expression; route two preselects one of resources/action/coordination and receives support through a “Meaning and Coordination” test; no extrapolation to other outcomes.","caption":"Based on A.3 and the testing of human empirical hypotheses in §4.2."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/figures/hv03-meaning.png","alt":"Shared wording does not establish shared inner states, and effects do not establish justification; heterogeneous objectives and refusal of representation must remain.","caption":"Based on A.3's protection, normative, and restoration items."}],"diagramBrief":"The first diagram fixes route one, route two, and the uniquely preselected outcome; the second distinguishes expression, effects, consent, and justification. Arrows indicate evidentiary responsibilities, not a compulsory process of shared meaning.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No actual “Meaning and Coordination” test was constructed; there is no causal estimate or formal determination of “supported.”","The representativeness, coercion risks, and heterogeneous objectives of real meaning-related material require safe evidence collection; analysts cannot project them."],"taskId":"S-A-3","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.3, Chinese source lines 3700–3765","claim_support":["All 39 HV03 fields, R0/R1, and protection of heterogeneous expression."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-H1-contract","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.2, Chinese source lines 586–601","claim_support":["H1's uniquely preselected outcome family and positive and null-conclusion checks."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ADA","title":"US National Archives: Statement By The President, July 26, 1990","url":"https://www.archives.gov/research/americans-with-disabilities/transcriptions/naid-6037493-statement-by-the-president-americans-with-disabilities-act-of-1990.html","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"1990-07-26","locator":"The statement's opening paragraph and final two body paragraphs, webpage lines 27 and 41–42","claim_support":["The 1990 signing statement publicly invokes freedom and equality, administrative support, and commitments to implementation."],"limits":["I read the complete statement transcript. This discussion relies only on the expression itself; it does not establish genuine public consent, fulfillment of commitments, or causal effects of expressions of meaning, and offers no current legal advice."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"A.3　指向锚点（HV03）; Chinese source lines 3950–4033; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/source/S-A-3.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-3/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"02c99134be11a9f245e63f2b74e02b37b079047cdbd9c6e5b8cf65fee97fa986","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-03.md"};function l(u,e,p,m,h,f){const t=o("SectionLesson");return i(),a("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=r('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-3" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.3</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Orientation Anchor (HV03) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-3" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.3 Orientation Anchor (HV03) {#section-a-3}&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="xk-gist"><span class="xk-gist__tag">In brief</span><span class="xk-gist__text">The variable “Orientation Anchor” retains sourced expressions of meaning and heterogeneous goals; only when the outcome preselected for a “Meaning and Coordination” test receives support is a bounded effective anchor determined. Effects prove neither shared inner states nor goal justification.</span></div><ol><li>Proposition and Scope of Application</li></ol><p>Proposition: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem may first be recorded as candidate material about meaning. A conditionally effective orientation anchor is determined, within the scope of a “Meaning and Coordination” test (§4.2), only if that test provides support on the preselected outcome for resource allocation, action choice, or coordination.</p><p>Scope of application: Human structures involving intentionality, coordination, or common problems.</p><p>Conditions for suspension: Do not determine a conditionally effective orientation anchor under route two when there are only slogans, an interpreter’s projections, or coerced uniformity of expression. Sourced expressions, diverse expressions, and risks of coercion may still be recorded under route one. Do not infer a common will from them.</p><ol start="2"><li>Grounds and Limits on Conclusions</li></ol><p>Inferential premises: Structural domain (§7.3.1).</p><p>Method requirements: Observation Position and Model Limits (§4.8.2); conditions for evidence (§2.5); conditions for sources (§2.7).</p><p>Domain restriction: None.</p><p>Objects of application: None.</p><p>Support routes:</p><p>Route one, candidate anchor. Conclusion level: Candidate description. Conditions: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem has traceable ways of being expressed and carried, but there is not yet an eligible “Meaning and Coordination” test. Further inferential premises: None. Further method requirements: None. Can conclude: Record candidate material about meaning, diverse expressions, disputes over representativeness, and the need for a further “Meaning and Coordination” test. Ceiling: Candidate expressions of meaning only. They must not be called an effective orientation anchor or a common will.</p><p>Route two, effective anchor. Conclusion level: Conditional effect. Conditions: A pre-registered “Meaning and Coordination” test receives support on the single criterion selected in advance among resource allocation, action choice, and coordination outcomes. Further inferential premises: This “Meaning and Coordination” test. Further method requirements: Conditions for causal claims (§2.4); Retaining Competing Explanations and Residuals (§4.8.4). Can conclude: Determine a conditionally effective orientation anchor within this test, outcome family, scale, and time window. Ceiling: Do not extrapolate to unselected resource, action, or coordination outcomes. Do not infer genuine consent or a unified inner state, or treat the result as grounds for imposing uniformity of meaning.</p><p>Can infer: Conditional directions of coordination and anchors of conflict.</p><p>Cannot infer: That a group has a unified inner state; that shared language amounts to genuine consent; that the goal is normatively justified.</p><ol start="3"><li>Scale and Object</li></ol><p>Nine-axis scale: Aggregation level covers individual expressions or actions, cases of particular subjects, the population of potential participants, distributions of stances, and aggregation rules. Spatial extent covers sites of relationships, organizational spaces, digital public spaces, and the reach of propagation across domains. Time span covers the window from expression to action, cycles of commitment, and lags in drift and decoupling. Organizational level covers roles in action, teams, organizations, institutions, and public governance ecologies. Causal level covers events of expression, mechanisms of interaction between meaning and action, meso-level coordination structures, institutional arrangements, and systemic conditions. Observation resolution covers original expressions and actions, event sequences, individual cases, distributions of stances, coordination metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. Scope of influence covers direct participants, dissenters, indirectly affected people, second-order consequences for coordination, and impacts across domains and generations. Network topology scope covers propagation of expressions, links for coordination, heterogeneous clusters, and bridging nodes. Scope of jurisdiction and authorization states the authorization for naming the anchor, adopting a judgment of representativeness, and requiring coordination or uniformity separately. Diverse anchors must be preserved.</p><p>Effective object: A goal, identity, memory, commitment, fear, or common problem that may change resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcomes.</p><p>Kept across scales: The bridge from meaning to the preselected resource allocation, action choice, or coordination outcome.</p><p>Must be added when moving to a larger scale: Rules of representation; heterogeneity; distribution of costs and benefits; scope of authorization.</p><p>Changes with scale: The content and strength of an anchor and the subjects who carry it can change.</p><p>Objects to which it does not apply: Nonhuman systems without intentionality, meaning, or capability for commitment.</p><p>Cannot be raised to: Local expression taken directly as a common will.</p><ol start="4"><li>State, Evidence, and Relations Between Variables</li></ol><p>States: Dispersed; coalescing; competing; solidified; decoupled.</p><p>Observables: Differences in resource allocation before and after the pre-registered expression of meaning appears; changes in action choices, rates of completion in collaboration, or conflict patterns; acceptance, rejection, and alternative expressions of the anchor at different positions; whether the outcome difference under the comparison conditions exceeds the predetermined threshold.</p><p>Evidence requirements: Resource adjustments; sequences of action; commitment and exit records; evidence of conflict.</p><p>Inputs: Positions of participation; safety of expression; data on resources and actions.</p><p>Outputs: Generative events (§7.2.3); mobilization of uptake; agendas for normative choice.</p><p>Time window and lag: Distinguish short-term slogans, long-term commitments, and intergenerational memory.</p><p>Uncertainty: Record silence, coerced agreement, and internal heterogeneity.</p><p>Local exclusion zone: Different goals at positions where people have limited safety must not be erased in aggregation.</p><p>Affected positions: People who identify with the anchor; dissenters; people represented; cost bearers.</p><ol start="5"><li>Uptake, Responsibility, Ceilings, and Correction</li></ol><p>Uptake carriers: Narratives; commitments; shared memory; institutional goals; problem definitions.</p><p>Responsible parties: Those making claims of representativeness; those allocating resources on that basis.</p><p>Normative status: The existence of an anchor does not establish that it is normatively justified.</p><p>Judgment ceiling: Limit the two routes separately. Route one only describes candidate expressions of meaning. When the corresponding “Meaning and Coordination” test supports the effect on the selected outcome, route two may explain a conditional anchor within a limited scope. Do not extrapolate to unselected outcomes.</p><p>Action ceiling: This variable produces only descriptions of candidate anchors, diverse expressions, comparison results, and needs for more evidence. It does not authorize unifying meaning, representing others’ wishes, or coordinating action. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass normative bridging (§4.14.1) and have in place the normative premises stated explicitly on this occasion, itemized authorization, and operational procedures.</p><p>Counterexamples: A repeated slogan does not change any allocation of resources or action; uniform expressions under high pressure conceal genuine goals that conflict.</p><p>Appeal: Under the common rule for appeal and restoration (§7.4), members can contest representativeness, put forward different goals, or refuse to be anchored through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection, and initiate a review independent of the original chain of judgment about the anchor.</p><p>Restoration: Under the same rule, once restoration is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline actually revoke the naming of the anchor, remove its force as a representation and its downstream effect on coordination, and restore the state of diverse expression. Changes leave a record, and their completion is verified.</p></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.3"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{v as __pageData,y as default};
